var e=[{topicId:`bc-enzymes`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`An enzyme is a biological catalyst, almost always a protein, that speeds up a reaction by lowering its activation energy without being consumed. It changes how fast equilibrium is reached, never the position of equilibrium.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Apoenzyme: the protein part alone, catalytically inactive.`,`Cofactor: the non-protein helper. It may be a metal ion (Zn2+, Mg2+) or an organic molecule.`,`Coenzyme: an organic cofactor, usually derived from a vitamin, that binds loosely and is released after the reaction (NAD+, CoA).`,`Prosthetic group: a cofactor bound tightly or covalently and never released (FAD in succinate dehydrogenase, haem in catalase).`,`Holoenzyme: apoenzyme plus cofactor, the complete active enzyme.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`The substrate binds to a small pocket called the active site, made of amino acid residues that may be far apart in the primary sequence but brought together by folding. The active site provides both binding and catalytic residues.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Lock and key model (Emil Fischer): the active site is rigid and pre-shaped for the substrate.`,`Induced fit model (Koshland): the active site moulds itself around the substrate on binding. This is the accepted model.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`What changes enzyme activity`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Temperature: rate roughly doubles per 10 degrees C until denaturation; optimum for human enzymes about 37 degrees C.`,`pH: each enzyme has an optimum. Pepsin about 1.5 to 2, salivary amylase about 6.8, trypsin about 8, alkaline phosphatase about 9 to 10.`,`Substrate concentration: rate rises then plateaus once every enzyme molecule is occupied.`,`Enzyme concentration: rate is directly proportional, provided substrate is in excess.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Specificity is what makes enzymes useful. Absolute specificity means one substrate only (urease acts only on urea). Group specificity means one type of bond or group (trypsin cleaves after lysine and arginine). Stereospecificity means only one optical or geometric isomer is accepted (L-amino acid oxidase, fumarase acting on the trans isomer).`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Enzyme activity units`,text:`One International Unit (IU) is the amount of enzyme converting 1 micromole of substrate per minute under defined conditions. The SI unit is the katal (kat), 1 mole of substrate per second. 1 IU = 16.67 nanokatal.`}]},{topicId:`bc-enzymes`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`GPAT loads this topic with two things: the six-class IUB numbering system and Michaelis-Menten kinetics with its Lineweaver-Burk transformation. Learn both to the point of arithmetic.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`IUB classification`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`EC class`,`Name`,`Reaction`,`Example`],rows:[[`1`,`Oxidoreductases`,`Transfer of electrons / H`,`Lactate dehydrogenase`],[`2`,`Transferases`,`Transfer of a group`,`Aminotransferase (ALT)`],[`3`,`Hydrolases`,`Bond cleavage using water`,`Trypsin, lipase, urease`],[`4`,`Lyases`,`Non-hydrolytic bond cleavage`,`Aldolase, fumarase`],[`5`,`Isomerases`,`Intramolecular rearrangement`,`Phosphohexose isomerase`],[`6`,`Ligases`,`Bond making, ATP driven`,`Pyruvate carboxylase`]],caption:`A seventh class, translocases (EC 7), was added later for membrane transport enzymes.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Order of the six classes`,text:`Over The Hill Like In Louisiana: Oxidoreductase, Transferase, Hydrolase, Lyase, Isomerase, Ligase.`},{kind:`para`,text:`The EC number has four digits, for example EC 1.1.1.27 for lactate dehydrogenase: class, subclass, sub-subclass and serial number.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Michaelis-Menten kinetics`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`v = (Vmax x [S]) / (Km + [S])`,caption:`v = initial velocity; Vmax = maximum velocity at saturating substrate; [S] = substrate concentration; Km = Michaelis constant, in mol/L.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Km is the substrate concentration at which v = Vmax/2. It has units of concentration.`,`Low Km means high affinity of enzyme for substrate; high Km means low affinity.`,`Km is independent of enzyme concentration; Vmax is directly proportional to enzyme concentration.`,`When [S] is much less than Km the reaction is first order in [S]; when [S] is much greater than Km it is zero order (saturated).`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`1/v = (Km/Vmax) x (1/[S]) + 1/Vmax`,caption:`Lineweaver-Burk (double reciprocal) form. Slope = Km/Vmax, y-intercept = 1/Vmax, x-intercept = -1/Km.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Reversible inhibition`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Competitive`,`Non-competitive`,`Uncompetitive`],rows:[[`Binds to`,`Free enzyme, active site`,`E and ES, allosteric site`,`ES complex only`],[`Apparent Km`,`Increased`,`Unchanged`,`Decreased`],[`Apparent Vmax`,`Unchanged`,`Decreased`,`Decreased`],[`Vmax/Km ratio`,`Decreased`,`Decreased`,`Unchanged`],[`Reversed by excess S`,`Yes`,`No`,`No`],[`LB lines meet at`,`Y-axis`,`X-axis`,`Nowhere (parallel)`],[`Example`,`Malonate on succinate DH`,`Heavy metals on -SH enzymes`,`Lithium on inositol monophosphatase`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Competitive inhibition and Vmax`,text:`Vmax is never reduced by a competitive inhibitor because enough substrate can always outcompete it. Only the apparent Km rises. If a question says Vmax fell, it cannot be purely competitive.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Irreversible inhibitors form covalent bonds: organophosphates on acetylcholinesterase (serine), aspirin on cyclooxygenase (acetylation), penicillin on transpeptidase, iodoacetate on cysteine residues. Suicide (mechanism-based) inhibitors such as allopurinol on xanthine oxidase are activated by the enzyme itself.`}]},{topicId:`bc-enzymes`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At this level you should be able to compute apparent constants, read a plot, and use isoenzyme patterns diagnostically.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Km(apparent) = Km x (1 + [I]/Ki)`,caption:`Competitive inhibition. [I] = inhibitor concentration; Ki = dissociation constant of the EI complex. Vmax is unchanged.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Vmax(apparent) = Vmax / (1 + [I]/Ki)`,caption:`Pure non-competitive inhibition. Km is unchanged; Vmax falls by the same factor.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`kcat = Vmax / [E]total   and   catalytic efficiency = kcat / Km`,caption:`kcat is the turnover number, molecules of substrate converted per enzyme molecule per second. kcat/Km at 10^8 to 10^9 per M per s means the enzyme is diffusion limited (catalytically perfect).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Km in the original Briggs-Haldane treatment equals (k-1 + k2)/k1; only when k2 is negligible does Km equal the true dissociation constant Ks.`,`Carbonic anhydrase has the highest known turnover number, of the order of 600000 per second.`,`Alternative linear plots: Eadie-Hofstee, v = -Km(v/[S]) + Vmax; Hanes-Woolf, [S]/v = [S]/Vmax + Km/Vmax.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Allosteric regulation`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Allosteric enzymes are oligomeric, have a separate regulatory site, and give a sigmoid (S-shaped) v versus [S] curve, so they do not obey Michaelis-Menten kinetics.`,`K-type (K-series) modulators change apparent Km with Vmax constant; V-type modulators change Vmax with Km constant.`,`Concerted or symmetry model (Monod, Wyman, Changeux): all subunits flip together between T and R states. Sequential model (Koshland, Nemethy, Filmer): subunits change one at a time.`,`Feedback inhibition is the classic example: ATP inhibits phosphofructokinase-1; CTP inhibits aspartate transcarbamoylase.`,`Other control modes: covalent modification (phosphorylation of glycogen phosphorylase), zymogen activation (trypsinogen to trypsin), induction and repression of synthesis, and compartmentation.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Isoenzymes in diagnosis`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Isoenzyme`,`Structure / source`,`Clinical use`],rows:[[`LDH-1 (H4)`,`Heart, red cells`,`Rises in myocardial infarction`],[`LDH-5 (M4)`,`Liver, skeletal muscle`,`Rises in hepatic damage`],[`CK-MB`,`Cardiac muscle`,`Early marker of infarction`],[`CK-MM`,`Skeletal muscle`,`Muscular dystrophy, trauma`],[`CK-BB`,`Brain, smooth muscle`,`Brain injury`],[`ALP (Regan)`,`Placental type`,`Tumour marker`]],caption:`LDH exists as five tetramers of H and M subunits: H4, H3M, H2M2, HM3, M4.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The LDH flip`,text:`Normally serum LDH-2 exceeds LDH-1. After myocardial infarction the ratio flips so that LDH-1 exceeds LDH-2. Cardiac troponin I and T are now the preferred markers, but the flipped ratio is still a standard exam fact.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Ribozymes and abzymes`,text:`Not every catalyst is a protein. Ribozymes are catalytic RNA molecules; peptidyl transferase activity of the ribosome resides in the 23S rRNA. Abzymes are catalytic antibodies.`}]},{topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Carbohydrate metabolism is the set of pathways that get energy out of glucose, store it, and remake it when food is short. Every pathway has a location, a rate-limiting enzyme and a net yield: learn those three for each.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Pathway`,`Location`,`Purpose`],rows:[[`Glycolysis`,`Cytosol`,`Glucose to pyruvate, makes ATP`],[`TCA cycle`,`Mitochondrial matrix`,`Oxidises acetyl-CoA to CO2`],[`HMP shunt`,`Cytosol`,`Makes NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate`],[`Gluconeogenesis`,`Cytosol and mitochondria`,`Makes glucose from non-carbohydrate`],[`Glycogenesis`,`Cytosol`,`Stores glucose as glycogen`],[`Glycogenolysis`,`Cytosol`,`Releases glucose from glycogen`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`Glycolysis, also called the Embden-Meyerhof pathway, splits one six-carbon glucose into two three-carbon pyruvate molecules. It needs no oxygen, which is why the red blood cell, which has no mitochondria, depends on it entirely.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Preparatory (investment) phase: 2 ATP are spent, glucose is phosphorylated and split.`,`Payoff phase: 4 ATP and 2 NADH are made, so the net anaerobic gain is 2 ATP.`,`Under anaerobic conditions pyruvate is reduced to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase, which regenerates NAD+ so glycolysis can continue.`,`Under aerobic conditions pyruvate enters mitochondria and is converted to acetyl-CoA by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Glycogen is the storage form of glucose in animals, a branched polymer of glucose with alpha 1,4 links in the chains and alpha 1,6 links at branch points. Liver glycogen maintains blood glucose for the whole body; muscle glycogen serves only the muscle itself.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Insulin is the storage hormone: it lowers blood glucose, promotes glycolysis, glycogenesis and lipogenesis.`,`Glucagon and adrenaline raise blood glucose: they promote glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.`,`Normal fasting plasma glucose is about 70 to 100 mg/dL.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Only the liver can release free glucose`,text:`Glucose-6-phosphatase is present in liver and kidney but absent from muscle. That is why muscle glycogen cannot contribute directly to blood glucose.`}]},{topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core is: which steps are irreversible, which enzyme is rate limiting, and how many ATP come out. Work through each pathway with those three questions.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Glycolysis: the three irreversible steps`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Glucose to glucose-6-phosphate by hexokinase (glucokinase in liver). Uses 1 ATP.`,`Fructose-6-phosphate to fructose-1,6-bisphosphate by phosphofructokinase-1. Uses 1 ATP. This is the rate-limiting step of glycolysis.`,`Phosphoenolpyruvate to pyruvate by pyruvate kinase. Yields 1 ATP.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`The two substrate-level phosphorylation steps of glycolysis are catalysed by phosphoglycerate kinase and pyruvate kinase. The only oxidation step is glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, which generates NADH.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Hexokinase`,`Glucokinase`],rows:[[`Tissue`,`All tissues`,`Liver, pancreatic beta cells`],[`Km for glucose`,`Low (about 0.05 mM)`,`High (about 10 mM)`],[`Affinity`,`High`,`Low`],[`Inhibited by G-6-P`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Induced by insulin`,`No`,`Yes`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Inhibitor`,`Enzyme blocked`,`Consequence`],rows:[[`Iodoacetate`,`Glyceraldehyde-3-P dehydrogenase`,`Blocks payoff phase`],[`Arsenate`,`Substitutes for Pi at GAPDH`,`Oxidation continues, no ATP`],[`Fluoride`,`Enolase`,`Used in blood glucose sample tubes`],[`Fluoroacetate`,`Aconitase (as fluorocitrate)`,`Blocks TCA cycle`],[`Arsenite`,`Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase`,`Binds lipoic acid -SH groups`],[`Malonate`,`Succinate dehydrogenase`,`Classic competitive inhibition`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`TCA cycle`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`One turn oxidises one acetyl-CoA and releases 2 CO2, 3 NADH, 1 FADH2 and 1 GTP.`,`Isocitrate dehydrogenase is the rate-limiting enzyme; citrate synthase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase are the other regulated points.`,`Succinate thiokinase (succinyl-CoA synthetase) is the only substrate-level phosphorylation of the cycle.`,`Succinate dehydrogenase is the only membrane-bound enzyme of the cycle and is identical to Complex II of the respiratory chain.`,`The cycle is amphibolic: it both oxidises fuel and supplies precursors for haem, amino acids and glucose.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Coenzymes of pyruvate dehydrogenase`,text:`Tender Loving Care For Nancy: Thiamine pyrophosphate, Lipoic acid, Coenzyme A, FAD, NAD+. The same five serve the alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase complexes.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`HMP shunt and gluconeogenesis`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`HMP shunt produces no ATP. Its products are NADPH for reductive biosynthesis and antioxidant defence, and ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotides.`,`Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase is the rate-limiting enzyme of the shunt and the only irreversible part; the non-oxidative phase (transketolase, needing TPP, and transaldolase) is fully reversible.`,`Active in liver, adipose tissue, adrenal cortex, red cells, lactating mammary gland and gonads; minimal in skeletal muscle.`,`Gluconeogenesis bypasses the three irreversible glycolytic steps using four enzymes: pyruvate carboxylase (biotin, mitochondrial), PEP carboxykinase, fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase (rate limiting) and glucose-6-phosphatase.`,`Substrates are lactate, glycerol, glucogenic amino acids and propionate. Acetyl-CoA from even-chain fatty acids cannot become glucose.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate`,text:`This is a regulator, not a pathway intermediate. It activates phosphofructokinase-1 and inhibits fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, so it switches the liver towards glycolysis in the fed state. Insulin raises it; glucagon lowers it.`}]},{topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions are usually arithmetic on ATP yields, an enzyme deficiency asked as a disease, or a shuttle detail. Fix the modern P/O values first and state which convention you are using.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`ATP per NADH = 2.5 ; ATP per FADH2 = 1.5`,caption:`Modern consensus values used by current editions of Harper and Lehninger. Older texts use 3 and 2, which shifts all totals upward.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Yield from 1 glucose`,`Modern (2.5/1.5)`,`Older (3/2)`],rows:[[`Anaerobic glycolysis, net`,`2 ATP`,`2 ATP`],[`Aerobic glycolysis, glycerophosphate shuttle`,`5 ATP`,`6 ATP`],[`Aerobic glycolysis, malate-aspartate shuttle`,`7 ATP`,`8 ATP`],[`Pyruvate to acetyl-CoA (x2)`,`5 ATP`,`6 ATP`],[`TCA cycle (x2 turns)`,`20 ATP`,`24 ATP`],[`Complete oxidation of glucose`,`30 to 32 ATP`,`36 to 38 ATP`]],caption:`The range 30 to 32 reflects which NADH shuttle the tissue uses.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Glycerol-3-phosphate shuttle: cytosolic NADH becomes mitochondrial FADH2, so it delivers only 1.5 ATP. Used by skeletal muscle and brain.`,`Malate-aspartate shuttle: preserves NADH inside the matrix, delivering 2.5 ATP. Used by liver, heart and kidney.`,`Gluconeogenesis from 2 pyruvate costs 6 high-energy phosphates: 4 ATP (2 pyruvate carboxylase, 2 phosphoglycerate kinase) and 2 GTP (PEPCK).`,`The Cori cycle returns muscle lactate to the liver for resynthesis into glucose: the muscle gains 2 ATP, the liver spends 6, so the cycle is a net energy loss borne by the liver.`,`The glucose-alanine cycle (Cahill cycle) carries both carbon and amino nitrogen from muscle to liver.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Glycogen storage diseases`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Eponym`,`Enzyme deficient`],rows:[[`I`,`von Gierke`,`Glucose-6-phosphatase`],[`II`,`Pompe`,`Lysosomal alpha-1,4-glucosidase`],[`III`,`Cori / Forbes`,`Debranching enzyme`],[`IV`,`Andersen`,`Branching enzyme`],[`V`,`McArdle`,`Muscle glycogen phosphorylase`],[`VI`,`Hers`,`Liver glycogen phosphorylase`],[`VII`,`Tarui`,`Muscle phosphofructokinase`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Rate-limiting enzymes of glycogen metabolism`,text:`Glycogen synthase for synthesis and glycogen phosphorylase for breakdown. Both are controlled by phosphorylation, but in opposite directions: phosphorylation activates phosphorylase and inactivates synthase. Glycogen phosphorylase requires pyridoxal phosphate.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Diagnostic thresholds commonly examined: fasting plasma glucose 126 mg/dL or above, 2-hour value in the oral glucose tolerance test 200 mg/dL or above, HbA1c 6.5 percent or above.`,`Renal threshold for glucose is about 180 mg/dL; glycosuria appears above it.`,`HbA1c reflects average glycaemia over roughly the preceding 8 to 12 weeks, the lifespan of the red cell.`,`Uncontrolled type 1 diabetes drives lipolysis and ketogenesis, giving diabetic ketoacidosis with a raised anion gap; type 2 more often gives a hyperosmolar non-ketotic state.`,`Chronic hyperglycaemia damages tissue through the polyol pathway (aldose reductase makes sorbitol, which is trapped in lens and nerve) and through non-enzymatic glycation forming advanced glycation end products.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Fructose and galactose disorders`,text:`Essential fructosuria (fructokinase deficiency) is harmless; hereditary fructose intolerance (aldolase B) is dangerous. Galactokinase deficiency causes cataract only; classical galactosaemia (galactose-1-phosphate uridyl transferase) causes liver and brain damage.`}]},{topicId:`bc-lipid`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Fatty acids are the densest fuel the body stores, giving about 9 kcal per gram against 4 for carbohydrate and protein. Lipid metabolism covers how they are broken down, made, converted to ketone bodies, and carried in blood.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Beta-oxidation: breakdown of fatty acids, occurs in the mitochondrial matrix, needs NAD+ and FAD.`,`Lipogenesis: synthesis of fatty acids, occurs in the cytosol, needs NADPH.`,`Ketogenesis: formation of ketone bodies from acetyl-CoA in liver mitochondria.`,`Lipolysis: hydrolysis of stored triacylglycerol in adipose tissue by hormone-sensitive lipase.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`A fatty acid must first be activated to its coenzyme A thioester by acyl-CoA synthetase (thiokinase) in the cytosol. This costs one ATP but two high-energy bonds, because ATP is split to AMP and pyrophosphate.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Long-chain acyl-CoA cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane on its own. Carnitine carries it across: carnitine palmitoyltransferase I on the outer membrane makes acylcarnitine, a translocase moves it in, and CPT-II regenerates acyl-CoA inside. Short and medium-chain fatty acids do not need this shuttle.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The three ketone bodies are acetoacetate, beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetone.`,`They are made in the liver but the liver cannot use them; brain, heart, skeletal muscle and kidney oxidise them.`,`In prolonged starvation the brain adapts to draw a large part of its energy from ketone bodies.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Cholesterol is a 27-carbon sterol made from acetyl-CoA. It is the precursor of bile acids, steroid hormones and vitamin D, and a structural component of membranes. Plants do not contain it.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Beta-hydroxybutyrate is not a ketone`,text:`Chemically it carries a hydroxyl group, not a keto group, so it does not respond to the nitroprusside (Rothera) test even though it is the most abundant ketone body in blood. Acetone is the one exhaled and responsible for the fruity breath.`}]},{topicId:`bc-lipid`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Beta-oxidation is a four-step spiral. Knowing the order of the four reactions lets you count ATP for any even-chain saturated fatty acid without memorising tables.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Oxidation by acyl-CoA dehydrogenase, producing FADH2.`,`Hydration by enoyl-CoA hydratase.`,`Oxidation by beta-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase, producing NADH.`,`Thiolytic cleavage by thiolase, releasing acetyl-CoA and an acyl-CoA two carbons shorter.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Cycles = (n/2) - 1 ; Acetyl-CoA formed = n/2`,caption:`n = number of carbons in a saturated even-chain fatty acid. Each cycle gives 1 FADH2 and 1 NADH.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Net ATP = 10(n/2) + 4[(n/2) - 1] - 2`,caption:`Using 2.5 ATP per NADH and 1.5 per FADH2, 10 ATP per acetyl-CoA in the TCA cycle, minus 2 for activation. For palmitate (n = 16) this gives 106 ATP.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Fatty acid`,`Cycles`,`Acetyl-CoA`,`ATP (2.5/1.5)`,`ATP (old 3/2)`],rows:[[`Palmitate C16`,`7`,`8`,`106`,`129`],[`Stearate C18`,`8`,`9`,`120`,`146`],[`Myristate C14`,`6`,`7`,`92`,`112`],[`Laurate C12`,`5`,`6`,`78`,`95`]],caption:`All values are net, after subtracting 2 high-energy phosphates for activation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ketone body metabolism`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`HMG-CoA synthase (mitochondrial isoform) is the rate-limiting enzyme of ketogenesis.`,`The liver lacks thiophorase (succinyl-CoA acetoacetyl-CoA transferase), so it cannot activate acetoacetate and cannot use ketone bodies.`,`Red cells cannot use ketone bodies either, because they have no mitochondria.`,`Ketosis arises whenever oxaloacetate is diverted to gluconeogenesis: starvation, uncontrolled diabetes, high-fat diet, prolonged vomiting.`,`Detection: Rothera nitroprusside test and dipsticks detect acetoacetate and acetone but not beta-hydroxybutyrate.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cholesterol biosynthesis`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`2 acetyl-CoA to acetoacetyl-CoA, then HMG-CoA (cytosolic HMG-CoA synthase).`,`HMG-CoA to mevalonate by HMG-CoA reductase, using 2 NADPH. Rate-limiting step, located in the endoplasmic reticulum.`,`Mevalonate to isopentenyl pyrophosphate, the 5-carbon isoprene unit.`,`Condensation to squalene (30 C), then cyclisation to lanosterol, then conversion to cholesterol.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Two HMG-CoA synthases`,text:`The mitochondrial enzyme feeds ketogenesis; the cytosolic enzyme feeds cholesterol synthesis. Same intermediate, two compartments, two fates.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Lipoprotein`,`Main apoprotein`,`Origin`,`Main cargo`],rows:[[`Chylomicron`,`B-48`,`Intestine`,`Dietary triacylglycerol`],[`VLDL`,`B-100`,`Liver`,`Endogenous triacylglycerol`],[`IDL`,`B-100, E`,`VLDL remnant`,`TG and cholesteryl ester`],[`LDL`,`B-100`,`From IDL`,`Cholesterol to tissues`],[`HDL`,`A-I`,`Liver, intestine`,`Cholesterol back to liver`]],caption:`Density rises HDL > LDL > IDL > VLDL > chylomicron; particle size falls in the same order.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Apoprotein functions`,text:`Apo C-II activates lipoprotein lipase, apo A-I activates LCAT, apo B-100 is the ligand for the LDL receptor, and apo E mediates hepatic uptake of remnants.`}]},{topicId:`bc-lipid`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The high-yield advanced material is odd-chain and unsaturated oxidation, the regulation of fatty acid synthesis, and the Fredrickson classification of hyperlipoproteinaemias.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Odd-chain fatty acids yield propionyl-CoA in the final cycle. It is carboxylated (biotin) to methylmalonyl-CoA and isomerised by methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (vitamin B12) to succinyl-CoA, which is glucogenic. This is the only route by which fatty acid carbon becomes glucose.`,`Unsaturated fatty acids need two extra enzymes, an isomerase (for odd-numbered double bonds) and a reductase requiring NADPH (for even-numbered ones), and yield slightly less ATP because a double bond replaces an acyl-CoA dehydrogenase step.`,`Alpha-oxidation occurs in peroxisomes and handles branched phytanic acid; its failure gives Refsum disease.`,`Omega-oxidation occurs in the endoplasmic reticulum and gives dicarboxylic acids, becoming important when beta-oxidation is impaired.`,`Very long chain fatty acids are shortened in peroxisomes first; failure of peroxisomal import causes Zellweger syndrome, and defective transport of VLCFA causes adrenoleukodystrophy.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Regulation of fatty acid synthesis`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Acetyl-CoA carboxylase is rate limiting. It needs biotin, ATP and bicarbonate and makes malonyl-CoA.`,`Citrate is the allosteric activator; long-chain palmitoyl-CoA is the feedback inhibitor. Phosphorylation by AMP-activated protein kinase inactivates it; insulin promotes dephosphorylation and activation.`,`Malonyl-CoA inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase I, which is how the fed state shuts off beta-oxidation while synthesis runs.`,`The multienzyme fatty acid synthase complex is a dimer; the product released is palmitate (C16). Elongation and desaturation occur afterwards in the endoplasmic reticulum.`,`NADPH for synthesis comes from the HMP shunt and from malic enzyme.`,`Humans cannot introduce a double bond beyond carbon 9, so linoleic acid and alpha-linolenic acid are essential.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Regulation of HMG-CoA reductase`,text:`Four controls: transcriptional induction through SREBP-2 when sterols are low, feedback inhibition by cholesterol and mevalonate, inactivation by AMPK phosphorylation, and competitive inhibition by statins, which resemble mevalonate.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Fredrickson type`,`Defect`,`Raised particle`],rows:[[`I`,`Lipoprotein lipase or apo C-II`,`Chylomicrons`],[`IIa`,`LDL receptor`,`LDL`],[`IIb`,`Overproduction of apo B`,`LDL and VLDL`],[`III`,`Abnormal apo E`,`IDL and remnants`],[`IV`,`Overproduction of VLDL`,`VLDL`],[`V`,`Mixed`,`VLDL and chylomicrons`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Fatty liver and lipotropic factors`,text:`Fat accumulates when triacylglycerol export as VLDL fails, usually from lack of phospholipid or apoprotein. Lipotropic factors that prevent it are choline, methionine, betaine, inositol and vitamin B12 and folate, all of which support phosphatidylcholine synthesis.`}]},{topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Unlike fat and carbohydrate, amino acids cannot be stored. Excess amino acid is deaminated, the carbon skeleton is burnt or converted to glucose or fat, and the nitrogen must be disposed of as urea.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Transamination: the amino group is handed from an amino acid to a keto acid. No ammonia is released.`,`Deamination: the amino group leaves as free ammonia.`,`Decarboxylation: the carboxyl group leaves as CO2, producing an amine such as histamine or GABA.`,`Transdeamination: transamination followed by oxidative deamination of glutamate, the main route for nitrogen removal.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Almost all transaminases use pyridoxal phosphate, the coenzyme form of vitamin B6. The two measured clinically are alanine aminotransferase (ALT, formerly SGPT) and aspartate aminotransferase (AST, formerly SGOT). ALT is the more liver-specific of the two.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Ammonia is toxic, especially to the brain. The liver converts it to urea, which is water soluble, non-toxic and excreted by the kidney. Urea contains two nitrogen atoms and is the major nitrogenous excretory product in humans.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Essential amino acids must come from the diet: phenylalanine, valine, threonine, tryptophan, isoleucine, methionine, histidine, leucine, lysine, with arginine semi-essential in growth.`,`Non-essential amino acids can be made from intermediates the body already has.`,`Nitrogen balance is positive in growth, pregnancy and recovery, and negative in starvation, burns, trauma and untreated diabetes.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Essential amino acids`,text:`PVT TIM HALL: Phenylalanine, Valine, Threonine, Tryptophan, Isoleucine, Methionine, Histidine, Arginine, Leucine, Lysine.`},{kind:`para`,text:`An inborn error of metabolism is an inherited deficiency of a single enzyme. The substrate before the block piles up and the product after it is missing, and the symptoms follow from those two facts.`}]},{topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The urea cycle, one-carbon metabolism and the classic aminoacidopathies are the three blocks that carry most of the marks here.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The urea cycle`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Step`,`Enzyme`,`Site`,`Note`],rows:[[`1`,`Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I`,`Mitochondria`,`Needs N-acetylglutamate, 2 ATP`],[`2`,`Ornithine transcarbamoylase`,`Mitochondria`,`Forms citrulline`],[`3`,`Argininosuccinate synthetase`,`Cytosol`,`Rate limiting, ATP to AMP`],[`4`,`Argininosuccinate lyase`,`Cytosol`,`Releases fumarate`],[`5`,`Arginase`,`Cytosol`,`Gives urea and ornithine`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`One nitrogen of urea comes from free ammonia through carbamoyl phosphate, the other from aspartate. The carbon comes from CO2 (bicarbonate).`,`Three ATP are consumed but four high-energy phosphate bonds are used, because step 3 splits ATP to AMP and pyrophosphate.`,`The cycle occurs only in the liver, spanning mitochondria and cytosol.`,`Fumarate released at step 4 links the urea cycle to the TCA cycle, the so-called Krebs bicycle.`,`N-acetylglutamate, made from glutamate and acetyl-CoA, is the obligatory allosteric activator of CPS-I and rises with a high-protein diet.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`CPS-I versus CPS-II`,text:`CPS-I is mitochondrial, uses ammonia as the nitrogen source, needs N-acetylglutamate and serves the urea cycle. CPS-II is cytosolic, uses glutamine, needs no activator and serves pyrimidine synthesis.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`One-carbon metabolism`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tetrahydrofolate carries one-carbon units at several oxidation levels: methyl, methylene, methenyl, formyl and formimino, attached at N5, N10 or bridging both.`,`Serine is the major donor of one-carbon units, through serine hydroxymethyltransferase; glycine, histidine (via formiminoglutamate) and tryptophan also contribute.`,`S-adenosylmethionine, made from methionine and ATP, is the universal methyl donor for methylation of DNA, noradrenaline to adrenaline, and creatine synthesis.`,`Vitamin B12 is required by methionine synthase to move the methyl group from N5-methyl-THF to homocysteine. Without B12 folate is trapped as N5-methyl-THF, the methyl-folate trap, which is why B12 deficiency produces a folate-deficiency type of megaloblastic anaemia.`,`The FIGLU excretion test after a histidine load is the classical indicator of folate deficiency.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Inborn errors of amino acid metabolism`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Disorder`,`Enzyme deficient`,`Key feature`],rows:[[`Phenylketonuria`,`Phenylalanine hydroxylase`,`Mousy urine, mental retardation`],[`Alkaptonuria`,`Homogentisate oxidase`,`Urine darkens on standing`],[`Maple syrup urine disease`,`Branched-chain keto acid DH`,`Sweet-smelling urine`],[`Albinism`,`Tyrosinase`,`No melanin`],[`Homocystinuria`,`Cystathionine beta-synthase`,`Lens dislocation, thrombosis`],[`Tyrosinaemia type I`,`Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase`,`Liver and renal damage`],[`Hartnup disease`,`Neutral amino acid transporter`,`Pellagra-like rash`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`PKU essentials`,text:`Phenylalanine hydroxylase needs tetrahydrobiopterin, so a BH4 defect gives a malignant variant. Phenylalanine is shunted to phenylpyruvate, phenyllactate and phenylacetate. Tyrosine becomes an essential amino acid. Screening is by the Guthrie bacterial inhibition assay or tandem mass spectrometry, and treatment is a phenylalanine-restricted diet.`}]},{topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions pick at fate of carbon skeletons, specialised products of individual amino acids, and the exact enzyme defect behind a hyperammonaemia.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Purely ketogenic amino acids: leucine and lysine only.`,`Both glucogenic and ketogenic: phenylalanine, tyrosine, tryptophan and isoleucine.`,`All the rest are purely glucogenic.`,`Amino acids that cannot undergo transamination: lysine and threonine.`,`Glutamate dehydrogenase is the main oxidative deaminating enzyme, uniquely able to use either NAD+ or NADP+; it is activated by ADP and inhibited by ATP and GTP.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Precursor amino acid`,`Product`],rows:[[`Tyrosine`,`Dopamine, noradrenaline, adrenaline, melanin, T3 and T4`],[`Tryptophan`,`Serotonin, melatonin, niacin`],[`Histidine`,`Histamine`],[`Glutamate`,`GABA, glutathione`],[`Arginine`,`Nitric oxide, creatine, urea`],[`Glycine`,`Haem, purines, creatine, bile salts`],[`Methionine`,`S-adenosylmethionine, cysteine`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Urea cycle defect`,`Enzyme`,`Marker`],rows:[[`Hyperammonaemia type I`,`CPS-I`,`Low citrulline`],[`Hyperammonaemia type II`,`Ornithine transcarbamoylase`,`High orotic acid, X-linked`],[`Citrullinaemia`,`Argininosuccinate synthetase`,`High citrulline`],[`Argininosuccinic aciduria`,`Argininosuccinate lyase`,`Argininosuccinate in urine`],[`Argininaemia`,`Arginase`,`High arginine`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why OTC deficiency raises orotic acid`,text:`Carbamoyl phosphate that cannot be used by the blocked urea cycle leaks into the cytosol and is diverted into pyrimidine synthesis, so orotic acid rises. This is the single distinguishing test between CPS-I and OTC deficiency, and OTC deficiency is the only X-linked urea cycle disorder.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Ammonia is transported from muscle as alanine and from brain and other tissues as glutamine; glutamine synthetase in brain consumes glutamate, which is thought to contribute to hepatic encephalopathy.`,`Homocystinuria is treated with pyridoxine in responsive patients, since cystathionine beta-synthase uses pyridoxal phosphate; raised homocysteine is an independent risk factor for atherosclerosis and thrombosis.`,`Ochronosis in alkaptonuria is the deposition of oxidised, polymerised homogentisic acid in cartilage, giving pigmented ears and degenerative arthritis in adult life.`,`Maple syrup urine disease blocks the branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex, which shares all five coenzymes with pyruvate dehydrogenase; thiamine-responsive variants exist.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Selenocysteine, the twenty-first amino acid`,text:`It is inserted co-translationally at a UGA codon read by a special tRNA in the presence of a SECIS element, not by post-translational modification. It is found in glutathione peroxidase and iodothyronine deiodinase.`}]},{topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`DNA stores the genetic information, RNA carries and translates it. Both are polymers of nucleotides, and the vocabulary of base, nucleoside and nucleotide has to be exact.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Base: the nitrogenous ring alone, for example adenine.`,`Nucleoside: base plus sugar, for example adenosine.`,`Nucleotide: base plus sugar plus phosphate, for example adenosine monophosphate.`,`Purines are double-ringed: adenine and guanine. Pyrimidines are single-ringed: cytosine, thymine and uracil.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Pyrimidines`,text:`CUT the Py: Cytosine, Uracil and Thymine are the pyrimidines. Thymine is in DNA only, uracil in RNA only.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Watson and Crick described DNA as a right-handed double helix of two antiparallel strands, held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary bases: adenine with thymine by two bonds, guanine with cytosine by three. Because G-C pairs have three bonds, DNA rich in G and C needs a higher temperature to melt.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`B-DNA is the physiological form: right handed, about 10 base pairs per turn, rise of 0.34 nm per base pair and pitch 3.4 nm.`,`Z-DNA is left handed with a zigzag backbone.`,`Chargaff rule: in any double-stranded DNA, A equals T and G equals C, so purines equal pyrimidines.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`The central dogma is the flow of information: DNA is copied to DNA in replication, transcribed to RNA, and translated to protein. Reverse transcriptase in retroviruses runs RNA back to DNA, the one legitimate exception.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`mRNA carries the coding message and is the least abundant class.`,`tRNA is the adaptor that brings amino acids; it is the smallest and has a cloverleaf secondary structure.`,`rRNA is structural and catalytic and is the most abundant RNA in the cell.`]}]},{topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`For GPAT, replication, transcription and translation are examined through their enzymes, their inhibitors, and the features of the genetic code.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Replication`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Semiconservative, proved by the Meselson-Stahl density experiment.`,`Synthesis is strictly 5 prime to 3 prime, so one strand is made continuously (leading) and the other in short Okazaki fragments (lagging).`,`Helicase unwinds, single-strand binding proteins hold the strands apart, topoisomerase and DNA gyrase relieve supercoiling, primase lays an RNA primer.`,`In prokaryotes DNA polymerase III elongates, DNA polymerase I removes the primer with its 5 prime to 3 prime exonuclease and fills the gap, DNA ligase seals the nick.`,`Proofreading is the 3 prime to 5 prime exonuclease activity of the polymerase.`,`Eukaryotic polymerases: alpha carries primase, delta and epsilon elongate, beta repairs, gamma copies mitochondrial DNA. Telomerase is a reverse transcriptase carrying its own RNA template.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Transcription`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`RNA is copied from the template (antisense) strand; the sequence of the coding strand matches the RNA except that T is replaced by U.`,`Prokaryotes use one RNA polymerase; the sigma factor recognises the promoter, and the Pribnow box at minus 10 has the consensus TATAAT.`,`Eukaryotes use three: RNA polymerase I makes rRNA, II makes mRNA and is inhibited by alpha-amanitin, III makes tRNA and 5S rRNA. The TATA or Hogness box sits around minus 25.`,`Eukaryotic mRNA is processed: a 7-methylguanosine cap at the 5 prime end, a poly-A tail at the 3 prime end, and removal of introns by the spliceosome following the GU-AG rule.`,`Rifampicin blocks the beta subunit of bacterial RNA polymerase; actinomycin D intercalates into DNA and blocks transcription in both.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The genetic code`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Triplet: 64 codons, of which 61 are sense codons and 3 are stop codons (UAA, UAG, UGA).`,`Degenerate: most amino acids have more than one codon. Methionine (AUG) and tryptophan (UGG) have only one each.`,`Non-overlapping, comma-less, and read 5 prime to 3 prime.`,`Nearly universal, with minor exceptions in mitochondria.`,`Wobble hypothesis: the third base of the codon pairs loosely, which is why degeneracy sits mostly in that position.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Target`,`Effect`],rows:[[`Streptomycin`,`30S subunit`,`Misreading of mRNA`],[`Tetracycline`,`30S A site`,`Blocks aminoacyl-tRNA binding`],[`Chloramphenicol`,`50S subunit`,`Inhibits peptidyl transferase`],[`Erythromycin`,`50S subunit`,`Blocks translocation`],[`Puromycin`,`Both 70S and 80S`,`Premature chain release`],[`Cycloheximide`,`60S subunit`,`Eukaryotic peptidyl transferase`],[`Diphtheria toxin`,`eEF-2`,`ADP-ribosylation, blocks translocation`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Initiator amino acid`,text:`Prokaryotes start with N-formylmethionine and use the Shine-Dalgarno sequence to position the ribosome; eukaryotes start with ordinary methionine and use cap-dependent scanning. Both use AUG.`}]},{topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Nucleotide biosynthesis is where the difficult marks are: the origin of every ring atom, the branch-point regulation, and the drugs that exploit each step.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Purine synthesis`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`De novo synthesis is cytosolic, mainly hepatic, and builds the ring on ribose-5-phosphate, so the first committed molecule already carries the sugar.`,`PRPP glutamyl amidotransferase is the rate-limiting enzyme; PRPP synthetase supplies its substrate.`,`Ring atoms: N1 from aspartate, C2 and C8 from formyl-tetrahydrofolate, N3 and N9 from glutamine, C4, C5 and N7 from glycine, C6 from CO2.`,`The first complete nucleotide formed is inosine monophosphate, which then branches to AMP and GMP.`,`The branch is reciprocal: GTP is required to make AMP and ATP is required to make GMP, so the two pools stay balanced.`,`Salvage uses HGPRT for hypoxanthine and guanine, and APRT for adenine, both consuming PRPP.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Purine degradation`,text:`Humans lack uricase, so the end product is uric acid, which is poorly soluble and causes gout. Xanthine oxidase, a molybdenum and FAD enzyme, catalyses the last two steps and is inhibited by allopurinol and febuxostat. Complete HGPRT deficiency gives Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, X-linked, with hyperuricaemia and self-mutilation; adenosine deaminase deficiency gives severe combined immunodeficiency.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Pyrimidine synthesis`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The ring is built first from carbamoyl phosphate and aspartate and only then attached to PRPP, the opposite order from purines.`,`Cytosolic carbamoyl phosphate synthetase II is the regulated step in humans; in bacteria aspartate transcarbamoylase is the classic regulated enzyme, inhibited by CTP and activated by ATP.`,`Orotate becomes OMP, then UMP, then UTP, and CTP synthetase converts UTP to CTP using glutamine.`,`Deoxyribonucleotides are made at the diphosphate level by ribonucleotide reductase using thioredoxin; hydroxyurea inhibits it.`,`Thymidylate synthase converts dUMP to dTMP using N5,N10-methylene-tetrahydrofolate, which is oxidised to dihydrofolate in the process and must be regenerated by dihydrofolate reductase.`,`Pyrimidine degradation gives soluble products, beta-alanine and beta-aminoisobutyrate, so it does not cause a gout-like disease.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Enzyme inhibited`,`Use`],rows:[[`Methotrexate`,`Dihydrofolate reductase`,`Anticancer, antirheumatic`],[`5-Fluorouracil`,`Thymidylate synthase`,`Anticancer`],[`Hydroxyurea`,`Ribonucleotide reductase`,`Anticancer`],[`6-Mercaptopurine`,`PRPP amidotransferase`,`Leukaemia`],[`Trimethoprim`,`Bacterial DHFR`,`Antibacterial`],[`Allopurinol`,`Xanthine oxidase`,`Gout`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Orotic aciduria`,text:`Hereditary orotic aciduria results from deficiency of UMP synthase and is treated with oral uridine, which bypasses the block and switches off CPS-II by feedback. Orotic aciduria with hyperammonaemia, however, points to ornithine transcarbamoylase deficiency instead.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Tm rises with (G + C) content`,caption:`Tm is the melting temperature at which half the DNA is denatured. G-C pairs have three hydrogen bonds against two in A-T, so a higher GC fraction raises Tm.`}]},{topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Vitamins are organic compounds needed in small amounts that the body cannot make in sufficient quantity. Most of the water-soluble ones work by becoming coenzymes, which is exactly how they are examined.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K need bile for absorption, are stored in the liver and adipose tissue, and can cause toxicity in excess.`,`Water-soluble vitamins, the B complex and vitamin C, are not stored to any great extent (vitamin B12 is the exception, stored in the liver for years) and excess is excreted in urine.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Biological oxidation is the controlled burning of fuel. Hydrogen atoms removed from substrates by dehydrogenases are handed to NAD+ or FAD, and those reduced coenzymes deliver electrons to the respiratory chain in the inner mitochondrial membrane.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The electron transport chain is a series of carriers of increasing electron affinity, ending with oxygen, which is reduced to water.`,`Energy released as electrons fall down the chain is used to pump protons out of the matrix.`,`Protons flowing back in through ATP synthase drive the making of ATP. This coupling of oxidation to phosphorylation is oxidative phosphorylation.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`The chemiosmotic theory of Peter Mitchell explains the link: the intermediate between oxidation and ATP synthesis is not a chemical compound but an electrochemical proton gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Where the chain lives`,text:`All four respiratory complexes and ATP synthase are in the inner mitochondrial membrane, which is folded into cristae to increase area. The outer membrane is freely permeable to small molecules; the matrix holds the TCA cycle enzymes.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Minerals are inorganic. Macrominerals such as calcium, phosphorus, sodium, potassium and magnesium are needed in gram amounts.`,`Trace elements such as iron, zinc, copper, iodine, selenium, manganese, molybdenum, fluoride and cobalt are needed in milligram or microgram amounts.`]}]},{topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Two tables carry this topic: vitamin to coenzyme to deficiency, and complex to inhibitor. Memorise them as tables, not as prose.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Vitamin`,`Active coenzyme form`,`Deficiency disease`],rows:[[`B1 Thiamine`,`Thiamine pyrophosphate`,`Beriberi, Wernicke-Korsakoff`],[`B2 Riboflavin`,`FMN and FAD`,`Cheilosis, angular stomatitis, glossitis`],[`B3 Niacin`,`NAD+ and NADP+`,`Pellagra: dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia`],[`B5 Pantothenic acid`,`Coenzyme A, acyl carrier protein`,`Burning feet syndrome`],[`B6 Pyridoxine`,`Pyridoxal phosphate`,`Neuropathy, sideroblastic anaemia`],[`B7 Biotin`,`Carboxybiotin (carboxylases)`,`Dermatitis, alopecia`],[`B9 Folic acid`,`Tetrahydrofolate`,`Megaloblastic anaemia, neural tube defects`],[`B12 Cobalamin`,`Methyl- and deoxyadenosyl-cobalamin`,`Pernicious anaemia, cord degeneration`],[`C Ascorbic acid`,`Cofactor, not a classic coenzyme`,`Scurvy`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Fat-soluble vitamin`,`Active form / role`,`Deficiency`],rows:[[`A`,`11-cis retinal in vision, retinoic acid`,`Night blindness, xerophthalmia`],[`D`,`1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol`,`Rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults`],[`E`,`Alpha-tocopherol, antioxidant`,`Haemolysis in newborns, neuropathy`],[`K`,`Gamma-carboxylation of glutamate`,`Bleeding, prolonged prothrombin time`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Vitamin K dependent factors`,text:`Clotting factors II, VII, IX and X plus proteins C and S. Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, which is why its effect is reversed by vitamin K. Mnemonic: 1972, for factors 10, 9, 7 and 2.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The respiratory chain`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Complex`,`Name`,`Prosthetic groups`,`Pumps protons`],rows:[[`I`,`NADH dehydrogenase`,`FMN, iron-sulphur centres`,`Yes`],[`II`,`Succinate dehydrogenase`,`FAD, iron-sulphur centres`,`No`],[`III`,`Cytochrome bc1`,`Cyt b, cyt c1, Rieske Fe-S`,`Yes`],[`IV`,`Cytochrome c oxidase`,`Cyt a, cyt a3, copper centres`,`Yes`],[`V`,`ATP synthase`,`F0 and F1 units`,`Uses the gradient`]],caption:`Coenzyme Q (ubiquinone) and cytochrome c are the two mobile carriers; only cytochrome c is a protein and it sits on the outer face of the inner membrane.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Electrons from NADH enter at Complex I; electrons from FADH2 (succinate, acyl-CoA dehydrogenase, glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase) enter at the coenzyme Q level, bypassing Complex I. That is why FADH2 yields less ATP.`,`Cytochrome c oxidase is the only complex that reacts directly with molecular oxygen.`,`Coenzyme Q is the only non-protein, lipid-soluble carrier and is the point at which several dehydrogenases converge.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Order of carriers`,text:`NADH to FMN to coenzyme Q to cytochrome b to c1 to c to a to a3 to oxygen. Note the odd ordering: cytochrome c1 comes before cytochrome c.`}]},{topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced material is quantitative bioenergetics plus the precise site of each poison. Inhibitors and uncouplers behave in opposite directions and are a favourite discrimination question.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`P/O ratio = ATP formed per atom of oxygen reduced`,caption:`NADH gives 2.5 and FADH2 gives 1.5 on modern values; older texts quote 3 and 2. Roughly 4 protons are needed per ATP exported, 3 for the synthase and 1 for phosphate and nucleotide transport.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`delta G naught prime = -n x F x delta E naught prime`,caption:`n = electrons transferred; F = Faraday constant, 96.5 kJ per volt per mole; delta E = difference in standard redox potential. For NADH (-0.32 V) to oxygen (+0.82 V), n = 2 and delta E = 1.14 V, giving about -220 kJ/mol.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Site`,`Inhibitors`],rows:[[`Complex I`,`Rotenone, amytal, piericidin A, MPTP`],[`Complex II`,`Malonate, carboxin, TTFA`],[`Complex III`,`Antimycin A, dimercaprol (BAL)`],[`Complex IV`,`Cyanide, carbon monoxide, azide, hydrogen sulphide`],[`ATP synthase`,`Oligomycin`],[`ATP-ADP translocase`,`Atractyloside`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Uncouplers dissipate the proton gradient without going through ATP synthase: 2,4-dinitrophenol, dinitrocresol, FCCP and CCCP, high-dose salicylates, free fatty acids and unconjugated bilirubin.`,`The physiological uncoupler is thermogenin (uncoupling protein 1) in brown adipose tissue, which generates heat rather than ATP in the newborn and in hibernating animals.`,`An uncoupler increases oxygen consumption and heat while ATP synthesis falls and the P/O ratio drops. An inhibitor of the chain stops both oxygen consumption and ATP synthesis.`,`Oligomycin blocks ATP synthase; oxygen uptake also stops because the gradient builds up and back-pressure halts electron flow. Adding an uncoupler restores oxygen uptake, which is the classic experiment.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Trace element`,`Key enzyme or protein`,`Disorder`],rows:[[`Iron`,`Haemoglobin, cytochromes, catalase`,`Microcytic anaemia`],[`Copper`,`Ceruloplasmin, cytochrome c oxidase, lysyl oxidase`,`Wilson disease, Menkes disease`],[`Zinc`,`Carbonic anhydrase, carboxypeptidase, alcohol DH`,`Acrodermatitis enteropathica`],[`Selenium`,`Glutathione peroxidase`,`Keshan disease, cardiomyopathy`],[`Iodine`,`Thyroxine and triiodothyronine`,`Goitre, cretinism`],[`Molybdenum`,`Xanthine oxidase, sulphite oxidase`,`Rare, neurological`],[`Cobalt`,`Vitamin B12 only`,`Seen as B12 deficiency`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Vitamin B12 absorption`,text:`B12 is bound in the stomach to intrinsic factor secreted by parietal cells and is absorbed in the terminal ileum. Deficiency therefore follows gastrectomy, autoimmune atrophic gastritis (pernicious anaemia), ileal resection and fish tapeworm infestation, not merely poor intake. Folate corrects the anaemia but does not stop the neurological damage.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Antioxidant defence`,text:`Superoxide dismutase needs copper and zinc in the cytosol and manganese in mitochondria; catalase is haem-based; glutathione peroxidase needs selenium and reduced glutathione, which is regenerated by glutathione reductase using NADPH from the HMP shunt. This is why G6PD deficiency causes oxidative haemolysis.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-bc-enzymes-1`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The tightly or covalently bound non-protein component of an enzyme that is not released during the reaction is called a`,options:[`Coenzyme`,`Prosthetic group`,`Apoenzyme`,`Holoenzyme`],answer:1,explanation:`A prosthetic group is a cofactor bound tightly or covalently and retained throughout catalysis, as FAD is in succinate dehydrogenase. A coenzyme, the tempting distractor, is also organic but binds loosely and dissociates after each catalytic cycle, like NAD+.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bc-enzymes-2`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Enzymes that catalyse the joining of two molecules coupled to hydrolysis of ATP belong to IUB class`,options:[`Class 4, lyases`,`Class 5, isomerases`,`Class 6, ligases`,`Class 2, transferases`],answer:2,explanation:`Ligases (class 6, also called synthetases) form new bonds using energy from ATP; pyruvate carboxylase is the standard example. Lyases are confused with them constantly, but lyases break bonds by non-hydrolytic elimination and require no ATP.`},{id:`q-bc-enzymes-3`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`For an enzyme with Km = 5 mmol/L and Vmax = 100 micromol/min, the velocity at a substrate concentration of 15 mmol/L is`,options:[`50 micromol/min`,`60 micromol/min`,`75 micromol/min`,`90 micromol/min`],answer:2,explanation:`Applying v = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S]) gives v = 100 x 15 / (5 + 15) = 1500/20 = 75 micromol/min. Choosing 50 means using [S] = Km, which is the half-Vmax point, not the value given.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bc-enzymes-4`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In a Lineweaver-Burk plot the y-intercept is 0.02 and the x-intercept is -0.25 (concentrations in mmol/L). Vmax and Km are`,options:[`Vmax 50, Km 4 mmol/L`,`Vmax 0.02, Km 0.25 mmol/L`,`Vmax 50, Km 0.25 mmol/L`,`Vmax 4, Km 50 mmol/L`],answer:0,explanation:`The y-intercept equals 1/Vmax, so Vmax = 1/0.02 = 50; the x-intercept equals -1/Km, so Km = 1/0.25 = 4 mmol/L. Reading the intercepts directly as the constants, without taking reciprocals, is the classic error.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bc-enzymes-5`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`An inhibitor that binds only to the enzyme-substrate complex will produce`,options:[`Increased Km, unchanged Vmax`,`Unchanged Km, decreased Vmax`,`Decreased Km and decreased Vmax`,`Increased Km and increased Vmax`],answer:2,explanation:`Binding exclusively to ES defines uncompetitive inhibition, which lowers both apparent Km and apparent Vmax by the same factor, so the Lineweaver-Burk lines are parallel. Increased Km with unchanged Vmax describes competitive inhibition, where the inhibitor binds free enzyme at the active site instead.`},{id:`q-bc-enzymes-6`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Malonate inhibition of succinate dehydrogenase is the standard example of`,options:[`Non-competitive inhibition`,`Competitive inhibition`,`Uncompetitive inhibition`,`Suicide inhibition`],answer:1,explanation:`Malonate is a structural analogue of succinate and competes for the same active site, so the inhibition is overcome by raising succinate concentration. Suicide inhibition would require the enzyme to convert the inhibitor into a reactive species that binds covalently, as happens with allopurinol and xanthine oxidase.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bc-enzymes-7`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`An enzyme has Km = 2 mmol/L. In the presence of a competitive inhibitor at 6 micromol/L with Ki = 3 micromol/L, the apparent Km becomes`,options:[`2 mmol/L`,`4 mmol/L`,`6 mmol/L`,`12 mmol/L`],answer:2,explanation:`Apparent Km = Km(1 + [I]/Ki) = 2(1 + 6/3) = 2 x 3 = 6 mmol/L. Answering 4 mmol/L comes from using the ratio [I]/Ki = 2 as the multiplier instead of 1 + [I]/Ki.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bc-enzymes-8`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which statement about an allosteric enzyme is correct?`,options:[`It gives a hyperbolic v versus [S] curve and obeys Michaelis-Menten kinetics`,`It is always a single polypeptide chain`,`A V-type modulator changes Vmax while Km stays constant`,`Its regulatory site is identical to the active site`],answer:2,explanation:`Allosteric enzymes are classified as K-type, altering apparent Km, or V-type, altering Vmax with Km unchanged. They are oligomeric with a regulatory site distinct from the active site and give a sigmoid curve, so they do not follow Michaelis-Menten kinetics, which rules out the first option.`},{id:`q-bc-carbohydrate-1`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis is`,options:[`Hexokinase`,`Phosphofructokinase-1`,`Pyruvate kinase`,`Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase`],answer:1,explanation:`Phosphofructokinase-1 catalyses the committed and slowest step and is the principal control point, inhibited by ATP and citrate and activated by fructose-2,6-bisphosphate. Hexokinase is also irreversible but is not rate limiting, since glucose-6-phosphate has several other fates.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bc-carbohydrate-2`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Net ATP yield from the anaerobic conversion of one molecule of glucose to two molecules of lactate is`,options:[`2 ATP`,`4 ATP`,`6 ATP`,`8 ATP`],answer:0,explanation:`Four ATP are generated in the payoff phase and two are consumed in the preparatory phase, giving a net of 2 ATP; the NADH produced is reoxidised by lactate dehydrogenase rather than by the respiratory chain. The answer 4 is the gross yield, not the net.`},{id:`q-bc-carbohydrate-3`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The only substrate-level phosphorylation of the TCA cycle is catalysed by`,options:[`Citrate synthase`,`Isocitrate dehydrogenase`,`Succinate thiokinase`,`Malate dehydrogenase`],answer:2,explanation:`Succinate thiokinase, also called succinyl-CoA synthetase, converts succinyl-CoA to succinate with formation of GTP, independent of the respiratory chain. Isocitrate dehydrogenase is the rate-limiting enzyme of the cycle but produces NADH, not a nucleoside triphosphate directly.`},{id:`q-bc-carbohydrate-4`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which set of features belongs to glucokinase rather than hexokinase?`,options:[`Low Km, present in all tissues, inhibited by glucose-6-phosphate`,`High Km, liver and pancreatic beta cells, induced by insulin`,`High Km, present in all tissues, inhibited by glucose-6-phosphate`,`Low Km, liver only, induced by glucagon`],answer:1,explanation:`Glucokinase has a high Km of about 10 mmol/L, is confined to liver and pancreatic beta cells, is not inhibited by its product and is induced by insulin, so it traps glucose only when portal concentrations are high. Low Km with product inhibition and universal distribution describes hexokinase.`},{id:`q-bc-carbohydrate-5`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The HMP shunt is important in the red blood cell mainly because it supplies`,options:[`ATP for the sodium pump`,`NADPH to keep glutathione reduced`,`2,3-bisphosphoglycerate`,`Ribose for nucleic acid synthesis`],answer:1,explanation:`NADPH from glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase regenerates reduced glutathione, which detoxifies peroxides and protects the mature red cell; deficiency of G6PD therefore causes oxidative haemolysis. The shunt yields no ATP at all, and the mature red cell makes no nucleic acid, so ribose is not the driver.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bc-carbohydrate-6`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which enzyme is absent from skeletal muscle, preventing it from contributing free glucose to blood?`,options:[`Glycogen phosphorylase`,`Phosphoglucomutase`,`Glucose-6-phosphatase`,`Debranching enzyme`],answer:2,explanation:`Glucose-6-phosphatase is confined to liver and kidney, so muscle glycogen can only be used within the muscle itself as glucose-6-phosphate. Muscle has an active glycogen phosphorylase, which is why its absence is not the explanation; McArdle disease is the condition where that muscle enzyme is missing.`},{id:`q-bc-carbohydrate-7`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`How many high-energy phosphate bonds are consumed in the synthesis of one molecule of glucose from two molecules of pyruvate?`,options:[`2`,`4`,`6`,`8`],answer:2,explanation:`Two pyruvate carboxylase steps use 2 ATP, two PEP carboxykinase steps use 2 GTP and two phosphoglycerate kinase steps use 2 ATP, giving 6 high-energy phosphates in total. The answer 4 omits the GTP used by PEPCK, which is the commonest slip.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bc-carbohydrate-8`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Von Gierke disease (glycogen storage disease type I) results from deficiency of`,options:[`Lysosomal alpha-1,4-glucosidase`,`Glucose-6-phosphatase`,`Muscle glycogen phosphorylase`,`Branching enzyme`],answer:1,explanation:`Type I is glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency, giving severe fasting hypoglycaemia, hepatomegaly, lactic acidosis and hyperuricaemia. Lysosomal alpha-1,4-glucosidase deficiency is Pompe disease, type II, which presents as cardiomegaly with normal blood glucose because cytosolic glycogen handling is intact.`},{id:`q-bc-lipid-1`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Beta-oxidation of fatty acids takes place in the`,options:[`Cytosol`,`Mitochondrial matrix`,`Endoplasmic reticulum`,`Peroxisome only`],answer:1,explanation:`The four-step beta-oxidation spiral operates in the mitochondrial matrix, which is why the carnitine shuttle is needed to get long-chain acyl-CoA in. The cytosol is where fatty acid synthesis and the initial activation to acyl-CoA occur, and peroxisomes handle only very long chain and branched fatty acids.`},{id:`q-bc-lipid-2`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The liver cannot oxidise ketone bodies because it lacks`,options:[`HMG-CoA synthase`,`Thiophorase (succinyl-CoA acetoacetyl-CoA transferase)`,`Beta-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase`,`Thiolase`],answer:1,explanation:`Thiophorase, which activates acetoacetate to acetoacetyl-CoA, is absent from liver, so ketone bodies made there must be exported to peripheral tissues. HMG-CoA synthase is present in liver and is in fact the rate-limiting enzyme of their synthesis, so its absence would abolish ketogenesis instead.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bc-lipid-3`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Complete oxidation of one molecule of palmitic acid (C16) yields how many cycles of beta-oxidation and how many acetyl-CoA?`,options:[`8 cycles and 8 acetyl-CoA`,`7 cycles and 8 acetyl-CoA`,`8 cycles and 7 acetyl-CoA`,`7 cycles and 7 acetyl-CoA`],answer:1,explanation:`For a saturated even-chain acid the number of cycles is (n/2) - 1 and the acetyl-CoA formed is n/2, so palmitate gives 7 cycles and 8 acetyl-CoA; the last cycle liberates two acetyl-CoA at once. Answering 8 cycles counts a cycle for the final four-carbon fragment that does not need one.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bc-lipid-4`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Using 2.5 ATP per NADH and 1.5 ATP per FADH2, the net ATP yield from complete oxidation of palmitic acid is`,options:[`96`,`106`,`129`,`131`],answer:1,explanation:`Seven FADH2 give 10.5, seven NADH give 17.5 and eight acetyl-CoA give 80, a gross 108, minus 2 high-energy phosphates for activation, so the net is 106 ATP. The value 129 is the same calculation on the older 3 and 2 convention, and 131 is that figure without subtracting the activation cost.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bc-lipid-5`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Malonyl-CoA regulates fat metabolism by inhibiting`,options:[`Acetyl-CoA carboxylase`,`Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I`,`HMG-CoA reductase`,`Hormone-sensitive lipase`],answer:1,explanation:`Malonyl-CoA, the first committed intermediate of fatty acid synthesis, inhibits CPT-I and so blocks entry of fatty acids into mitochondria, preventing a futile cycle of simultaneous synthesis and oxidation. Acetyl-CoA carboxylase is the enzyme that makes malonyl-CoA, not its target.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bc-lipid-6`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which apoprotein activates lipoprotein lipase?`,options:[`Apo A-I`,`Apo B-100`,`Apo C-II`,`Apo E`],answer:2,explanation:`Apo C-II, transferred to chylomicrons and VLDL from HDL, is the obligatory activator of lipoprotein lipase on the capillary endothelium; its deficiency mimics type I hyperlipoproteinaemia. Apo A-I is the activator of LCAT, a different enzyme working on HDL cholesterol.`},{id:`q-bc-lipid-7`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Statins lower cholesterol by competitively inhibiting an enzyme located in the`,options:[`Mitochondrial matrix, converting acetoacetyl-CoA to HMG-CoA`,`Endoplasmic reticulum, converting HMG-CoA to mevalonate`,`Cytosol, converting squalene to lanosterol`,`Endoplasmic reticulum, converting mevalonate to isopentenyl pyrophosphate`],answer:1,explanation:`HMG-CoA reductase is an endoplasmic reticulum enzyme that reduces HMG-CoA to mevalonate using 2 NADPH, and it is the rate-limiting step that statins mimic structurally. The mitochondrial HMG-CoA pathway is the ketogenic one and is untouched by statins, which is a favourite discriminator.`},{id:`q-bc-lipid-8`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Oxidation of an odd-chain fatty acid can contribute to gluconeogenesis because the final cycle yields`,options:[`Acetyl-CoA`,`Acetoacetyl-CoA`,`Propionyl-CoA`,`Malonyl-CoA`],answer:2,explanation:`The last thiolytic cleavage of an odd-chain acid gives propionyl-CoA, which is carboxylated using biotin and then isomerised by the vitamin B12 dependent methylmalonyl-CoA mutase to succinyl-CoA, a TCA intermediate that can leave as oxaloacetate. Acetyl-CoA, the product of every other cycle, cannot yield net glucose in humans.`},{id:`q-bc-protein-aminoacid-1`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The coenzyme required by all transaminases is`,options:[`Thiamine pyrophosphate`,`Pyridoxal phosphate`,`Biotin`,`Tetrahydrofolate`],answer:1,explanation:`Pyridoxal phosphate, derived from vitamin B6, forms the Schiff base intermediate that carries the amino group during transamination. Biotin is the carboxylation coenzyme and tetrahydrofolate carries one-carbon units, neither of which handles amino group transfer.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bc-protein-aminoacid-2`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The two nitrogen atoms of urea are derived from`,options:[`Two molecules of ammonia`,`Ammonia and aspartate`,`Ammonia and glutamine`,`Aspartate and glutamate`],answer:1,explanation:`The first nitrogen enters as free ammonia through carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I, and the second is donated by aspartate at the argininosuccinate synthetase step. Glutamine is the nitrogen donor for pyrimidine synthesis by CPS-II, not for the urea cycle.`},{id:`q-bc-protein-aminoacid-3`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The rate-limiting enzyme of the urea cycle is`,options:[`Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I`,`Ornithine transcarbamoylase`,`Argininosuccinate synthetase`,`Arginase`],answer:0,explanation:`Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I catalyses the committed, rate-limiting step and is the one point of allosteric control, being switched on by N-acetylglutamate — which itself rises when arginine and glutamate accumulate, so the cycle speeds up exactly when nitrogen load demands it. This is the answer every standard text expects. Argininosuccinate synthetase is the tempting alternative because some flux-control studies assign it the highest control coefficient, and it is the step that splits ATP to AMP plus pyrophosphate, but it is not the regulated step. Ornithine transcarbamoylase deficiency is the commonest urea cycle disorder, and arginase catalyses the final release of urea.`},{id:`q-bc-protein-aminoacid-4`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A neonate has urine that darkens on standing and later develops pigmented cartilage and arthritis. The deficient enzyme is`,options:[`Phenylalanine hydroxylase`,`Tyrosinase`,`Homogentisate oxidase`,`Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase`],answer:2,explanation:`Alkaptonuria is deficiency of homogentisate oxidase, so homogentisic acid accumulates, oxidises and polymerises to a dark pigment in urine and in cartilage, giving ochronosis. Phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency is phenylketonuria, in which the urine has a mousy odour and does not darken.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bc-protein-aminoacid-5`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Maple syrup urine disease is caused by a defect in the metabolism of`,options:[`Aromatic amino acids`,`Branched-chain amino acids`,`Sulphur-containing amino acids`,`Basic amino acids`],answer:1,explanation:`The branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex is deficient, so leucine, isoleucine and valine and their keto acids accumulate and give the urine its characteristic sweet smell. Aromatic amino acid defects give phenylketonuria, alkaptonuria and tyrosinaemia, which have quite different urinary findings.`},{id:`q-bc-protein-aminoacid-6`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The universal methyl group donor in biological methylation reactions is`,options:[`N5-methyl tetrahydrofolate`,`S-adenosylmethionine`,`Methylcobalamin`,`Betaine`],answer:1,explanation:`S-adenosylmethionine, formed from methionine and ATP, carries a high-energy sulphonium methyl group that it donates to DNA, noradrenaline, guanidinoacetate and many other acceptors. N5-methyl-THF and methylcobalamin sit upstream, serving only to remethylate homocysteine back to methionine.`},{id:`q-bc-protein-aminoacid-7`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which pair of amino acids is purely ketogenic?`,options:[`Leucine and lysine`,`Phenylalanine and tyrosine`,`Isoleucine and threonine`,`Tryptophan and valine`],answer:0,explanation:`Only leucine and lysine yield exclusively acetyl-CoA or acetoacetyl-CoA and can never give net glucose. Phenylalanine, tyrosine, tryptophan and isoleucine are both glucogenic and ketogenic, which is why they are such a persistent distractor.`},{id:`q-bc-protein-aminoacid-8`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Hyperammonaemia with markedly raised urinary orotic acid points to deficiency of`,options:[`Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I`,`Ornithine transcarbamoylase`,`Argininosuccinate lyase`,`UMP synthase`],answer:1,explanation:`When ornithine transcarbamoylase is deficient, mitochondrial carbamoyl phosphate escapes to the cytosol and is diverted into pyrimidine synthesis, so orotic acid rises alongside ammonia; it is also the only X-linked urea cycle disorder. UMP synthase deficiency also raises orotic acid but produces megaloblastic anaemia with normal ammonia.`},{id:`q-bc-nucleic-acid-1`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which base is found in RNA but not in DNA?`,options:[`Thymine`,`Uracil`,`Guanine`,`Cytosine`],answer:1,explanation:`Uracil replaces thymine in RNA; thymine is 5-methyluracil and is restricted to DNA, where the methyl group helps repair systems recognise deaminated cytosine. Guanine and cytosine occur in both nucleic acids.`},{id:`q-bc-nucleic-acid-2`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`How many of the 64 codons of the genetic code are stop (nonsense) codons?`,options:[`1`,`2`,`3`,`4`],answer:2,explanation:`UAA, UAG and UGA are the three termination codons, leaving 61 sense codons for the 20 amino acids, which is the basis of degeneracy. There is only one initiation codon, AUG, which is where the answer 1 comes from.`},{id:`q-bc-nucleic-acid-3`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In prokaryotes the RNA primer laid down during replication is removed by`,options:[`DNA polymerase I`,`DNA polymerase III`,`DNA ligase`,`Primase`],answer:0,explanation:`DNA polymerase I uses its 5 prime to 3 prime exonuclease activity to excise the RNA primer and simultaneously fills the gap with DNA, after which ligase seals the nick. DNA polymerase III is the main elongating enzyme and has no 5 prime to 3 prime exonuclease activity.`},{id:`q-bc-nucleic-acid-4`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Alpha-amanitin from the death cap mushroom selectively inhibits`,options:[`Bacterial RNA polymerase`,`Eukaryotic RNA polymerase I`,`Eukaryotic RNA polymerase II`,`Reverse transcriptase`],answer:2,explanation:`Alpha-amanitin is most sensitive against RNA polymerase II, which transcribes mRNA, so protein synthesis collapses; polymerase III is inhibited only at much higher concentrations and polymerase I is essentially resistant. Rifampicin is the agent that targets bacterial RNA polymerase.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bc-nucleic-acid-5`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The first nucleotide formed complete in the de novo purine pathway is`,options:[`AMP`,`GMP`,`Inosine monophosphate`,`Orotidine monophosphate`],answer:2,explanation:`The purine ring is assembled directly on ribose-5-phosphate and the first fully formed nucleotide is IMP, which then branches to AMP and GMP. Orotidine monophosphate belongs to the pyrimidine pathway, where the ring is built first and only afterwards joined to PRPP.`},{id:`q-bc-nucleic-acid-6`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Lesch-Nyhan syndrome results from complete deficiency of`,options:[`Adenine phosphoribosyltransferase`,`Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase`,`Xanthine oxidase`,`Adenosine deaminase`],answer:1,explanation:`Loss of HGPRT abolishes salvage of hypoxanthine and guanine, so PRPP accumulates, de novo synthesis accelerates and uric acid rises, with the characteristic self-mutilating behaviour in this X-linked disorder. Adenosine deaminase deficiency also disturbs purine handling but presents as severe combined immunodeficiency instead.`},{id:`q-bc-nucleic-acid-7`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`5-Fluorouracil kills dividing cells chiefly by inhibiting`,options:[`Dihydrofolate reductase`,`Thymidylate synthase`,`Ribonucleotide reductase`,`DNA polymerase`],answer:1,explanation:`Its active metabolite fluorodeoxyuridine monophosphate forms a stable ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and N5,N10-methylene-THF, halting dTMP formation and causing thymineless death. Methotrexate is the DHFR inhibitor, and although both starve the cell of thymidylate, the enzymes targeted are different.`},{id:`q-bc-nucleic-acid-8`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which statement about the wobble hypothesis is correct?`,options:[`It explains why the first base of the codon is degenerate`,`It allows one tRNA to read more than one codon through loose pairing at the third base`,`It applies to pairing between mRNA and rRNA`,`It requires exactly 61 different tRNA molecules`],answer:1,explanation:`Crick proposed that the third codon base pairs less strictly with the first anticodon base, so a single tRNA can recognise several synonymous codons and fewer than 61 tRNA species are needed. Degeneracy sits in the third position, not the first, which is why the first option is wrong.`},{id:`q-bc-vitamins-minerals-1`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The coenzyme form of thiamine is`,options:[`Thiamine pyrophosphate`,`Flavin adenine dinucleotide`,`Pyridoxal phosphate`,`Coenzyme A`],answer:0,explanation:`Thiamine is phosphorylated to thiamine pyrophosphate, the coenzyme of pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase and the branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase. FAD is the coenzyme of riboflavin and is a different vitamin altogether.`},{id:`q-bc-vitamins-minerals-2`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Cyanide is lethal because it inhibits`,options:[`Complex I`,`Complex II`,`Complex III`,`Complex IV`],answer:3,explanation:`Cyanide binds the ferric form of cytochrome a3 in cytochrome c oxidase, Complex IV, blocking the transfer of electrons to oxygen so that cells cannot use oxygen even when it is plentiful. Rotenone is the Complex I inhibitor and antimycin A acts at Complex III.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bc-vitamins-minerals-3`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`FADH2 yields fewer ATP than NADH because`,options:[`It carries only one electron`,`It donates electrons at the coenzyme Q level, bypassing one proton-pumping complex`,`It is oxidised in the cytosol`,`It cannot reach cytochrome c oxidase`],answer:1,explanation:`FADH2 feeds electrons in at ubiquinone, skipping Complex I, so fewer protons are pumped and the P/O ratio is 1.5 instead of 2.5. Both carriers deliver two electrons and both ultimately reduce oxygen at Complex IV, so the other options are factually wrong.`},{id:`q-bc-vitamins-minerals-4`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which agent is an uncoupler rather than an inhibitor of oxidative phosphorylation?`,options:[`Oligomycin`,`Antimycin A`,`2,4-Dinitrophenol`,`Atractyloside`],answer:2,explanation:`2,4-Dinitrophenol is a lipid-soluble proton carrier that discharges the gradient, so oxygen consumption and heat production rise while ATP synthesis falls. Oligomycin, by contrast, blocks the proton channel of ATP synthase and stops oxygen uptake as well, which is why it is an inhibitor and not an uncoupler.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-bc-vitamins-minerals-5`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Vitamin K is required for the post-translational modification of clotting factors, specifically for`,options:[`Hydroxylation of proline residues`,`Gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues`,`Phosphorylation of serine residues`,`Glycosylation of asparagine residues`],answer:1,explanation:`Vitamin K is the cofactor of gamma-glutamyl carboxylase, which creates the calcium-binding gamma-carboxyglutamate residues of factors II, VII, IX and X and proteins C and S. Proline hydroxylation belongs to collagen synthesis and needs vitamin C, which is the usual competing answer.`},{id:`q-bc-vitamins-minerals-6`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Deficiency of which vitamin produces peripheral neuropathy during isoniazid therapy?`,options:[`Thiamine`,`Riboflavin`,`Pyridoxine`,`Niacin`],answer:2,explanation:`Isoniazid forms a hydrazone with pyridoxal phosphate and increases its urinary loss, so pyridoxine is co-prescribed to prevent neuropathy. Isoniazid can also precipitate pellagra by disturbing tryptophan to niacin conversion, but the classic prophylaxis given with the drug is vitamin B6.`},{id:`q-bc-vitamins-minerals-7`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Using delta G = -nF delta E, the free energy change for electron transfer from NADH (E = -0.32 V) to oxygen (E = +0.82 V) with F = 96.5 kJ per volt per mole is approximately`,options:[`-110 kJ/mol`,`-160 kJ/mol`,`-220 kJ/mol`,`-440 kJ/mol`],answer:2,explanation:`delta E = 0.82 - (-0.32) = 1.14 V and n = 2, so delta G = -2 x 96.5 x 1.14, which is about -220 kJ/mol. Using n = 1 gives the -110 figure, and forgetting that a pair of electrons is transferred is the usual mistake.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-bc-vitamins-minerals-8`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Vitamin B12 deficiency causes accumulation of methylmalonic acid because B12 is the coenzyme for`,options:[`Methionine synthase`,`Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase`,`Propionyl-CoA carboxylase`,`Homocysteine methyltransferase`],answer:1,explanation:`Deoxyadenosylcobalamin is the coenzyme of methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, so its failure raises methylmalonic acid and is thought to underlie the neurological damage. Methionine synthase, the other B12 enzyme, uses methylcobalamin and its failure raises homocysteine, not methylmalonate, which is how the two deficiencies are separated from folate deficiency.`}],n=[{id:`f-bc-enzymes-1`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,front:`What does Km represent?`,back:`The substrate concentration at which velocity is half of Vmax. Low Km means high affinity.`},{id:`f-bc-enzymes-2`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,front:`Lineweaver-Burk equation and its intercepts?`,back:`1/v = (Km/Vmax)(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax. Y-intercept 1/Vmax, x-intercept -1/Km, slope Km/Vmax.`},{id:`f-bc-enzymes-3`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,front:`Effect of a competitive inhibitor on Km and Vmax?`,back:`Km increases, Vmax unchanged. Lineweaver-Burk lines meet on the y-axis.`},{id:`f-bc-enzymes-4`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,front:`The six IUB enzyme classes in order?`,back:`Oxidoreductases, transferases, hydrolases, lyases, isomerases, ligases.`},{id:`f-bc-enzymes-5`,topicId:`bc-enzymes`,front:`Which LDH isoenzyme rises first in myocardial infarction?`,back:`LDH-1 (H4), giving the flipped LDH-1 greater than LDH-2 ratio.`},{id:`f-bc-carbohydrate-1`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,front:`The three irreversible enzymes of glycolysis?`,back:`Hexokinase (or glucokinase), phosphofructokinase-1 and pyruvate kinase.`},{id:`f-bc-carbohydrate-2`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,front:`Products of one turn of the TCA cycle?`,back:`3 NADH, 1 FADH2, 1 GTP and 2 CO2, equal to 10 ATP on modern values.`},{id:`f-bc-carbohydrate-3`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,front:`Rate-limiting enzyme of the HMP shunt?`,back:`Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase. The shunt makes NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate, no ATP.`},{id:`f-bc-carbohydrate-4`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,front:`The four key enzymes unique to gluconeogenesis?`,back:`Pyruvate carboxylase, PEP carboxykinase, fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase and glucose-6-phosphatase.`},{id:`f-bc-carbohydrate-5`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,front:`What does the Cori cycle carry, and at what cost?`,back:`Lactate from muscle to liver for resynthesis to glucose; muscle gains 2 ATP, liver spends 6.`},{id:`f-bc-carbohydrate-6`,topicId:`bc-carbohydrate`,front:`Total ATP from complete oxidation of one glucose?`,back:`30 to 32 ATP on modern values (36 to 38 on the older 3 and 2 convention).`},{id:`f-bc-lipid-1`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,front:`Net ATP from complete oxidation of palmitate?`,back:`106 ATP using 2.5 per NADH and 1.5 per FADH2 (129 on the older convention).`},{id:`f-bc-lipid-2`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,front:`Rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis?`,back:`HMG-CoA reductase, in the endoplasmic reticulum, inhibited by statins.`},{id:`f-bc-lipid-3`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,front:`Which enzyme controls entry of fatty acids into mitochondria?`,back:`Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I, inhibited by malonyl-CoA.`},{id:`f-bc-lipid-4`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,front:`Name the three ketone bodies.`,back:`Acetoacetate, beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetone.`},{id:`f-bc-lipid-5`,topicId:`bc-lipid`,front:`Which lipoprotein carries dietary triacylglycerol and which apoprotein marks it?`,back:`Chylomicron, marked by apo B-48.`},{id:`f-bc-protein-aminoacid-1`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,front:`Rate-limiting enzyme of the urea cycle?`,back:`Argininosuccinate synthetase, in the cytosol.`},{id:`f-bc-protein-aminoacid-2`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,front:`How many ATP and how many high-energy bonds does one urea cost?`,back:`3 ATP consumed but 4 high-energy phosphate bonds used.`},{id:`f-bc-protein-aminoacid-3`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,front:`Enzyme deficient in phenylketonuria and its cofactor?`,back:`Phenylalanine hydroxylase, requiring tetrahydrobiopterin.`},{id:`f-bc-protein-aminoacid-4`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,front:`Which amino acids are purely ketogenic?`,back:`Leucine and lysine only.`},{id:`f-bc-protein-aminoacid-5`,topicId:`bc-protein-aminoacid`,front:`What is the methyl-folate trap?`,back:`In B12 deficiency folate is stuck as N5-methyl-THF, giving megaloblastic anaemia despite adequate folate.`},{id:`f-bc-nucleic-acid-1`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,front:`Rate-limiting enzyme of de novo purine synthesis?`,back:`PRPP glutamyl amidotransferase.`},{id:`f-bc-nucleic-acid-2`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,front:`End product of purine degradation in humans?`,back:`Uric acid, because humans lack uricase.`},{id:`f-bc-nucleic-acid-3`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,front:`The three stop codons?`,back:`UAA, UAG and UGA.`},{id:`f-bc-nucleic-acid-4`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,front:`Which eukaryotic RNA polymerase makes mRNA and what inhibits it?`,back:`RNA polymerase II, inhibited by alpha-amanitin.`},{id:`f-bc-nucleic-acid-5`,topicId:`bc-nucleic-acid`,front:`Which enzyme converts dUMP to dTMP and which drug blocks it?`,back:`Thymidylate synthase, blocked by 5-fluorouracil.`},{id:`f-bc-vitamins-minerals-1`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,front:`P/O ratio for NADH and for FADH2?`,back:`2.5 and 1.5 on modern values (3 and 2 on the older convention).`},{id:`f-bc-vitamins-minerals-2`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,front:`Which complexes of the respiratory chain pump protons?`,back:`Complexes I, III and IV. Complex II does not.`},{id:`f-bc-vitamins-minerals-3`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,front:`Name three uncouplers of oxidative phosphorylation.`,back:`2,4-dinitrophenol, high-dose salicylate and thermogenin (UCP-1) in brown fat.`},{id:`f-bc-vitamins-minerals-4`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,front:`Coenzyme forms of vitamin B12?`,back:`Methylcobalamin for methionine synthase and deoxyadenosylcobalamin for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase.`},{id:`f-bc-vitamins-minerals-5`,topicId:`bc-vitamins-minerals`,front:`Deficiency disease of niacin and its coenzymes?`,back:`Pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia); coenzymes NAD+ and NADP+.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};